<!--
  Writing Journal 2 — ENGL 1010 First-Year Composition SBA (Activity bank)

  This one file serves both halves of Writing Journal 2. In 2a (the group chat)
  the conversation (writing_journal2_dialogue_part1.chatpeg) pulls each screen
  below into the right-hand pane via `wj2_sidebar -> <id>` arrows. In 2b (the
  in-person meeting) the single wj2_meeting screen at the bottom is shown
  full-width, with no chat. Read-only / framing screens are wrapped in IntakeGate
  so they page through with NEXT and drop out of the final PDF; the student's own
  work stays.

  Flow (from the SBA_Writing_Journal2_RevisedMBain design document + the
  reviewer comments in it):
    intro slide
    -> writer's-block debate: your quick take (activates prior experience)
    -> TASK 1a: "How do you really feel about writing?" quiz (26 statements, 3 rounds)
    -> TASK 1b: "What makes a good writer?" priorities (3 rounds)
    -> RESULTS: the Circle's tool plots you on three attitude continuums
    -> reflect on your placement (does it fit? a moment that explains it? a year from now?)
    == end of Writing Journal 2a; the rest is Writing Journal 2b, in person ==
    -> listen to three peers' stories (receptive / modeling)
    -> TASK 2: your turn — write OR record the story of an experience that shaped you
    -> the tool reflects your story back and builds on your attitude profile
    -> Print &amp; Submit (the print page: confirm, export, hand in).

  Reviewer comments addressed:
    C7  give the student the option to write OR record  -> optional freewrite warm-up + a
        written entry, with a note on recording (no audio-capture block exists in lo-blocks).
    C8  characters model the kind of story first        -> the three peer stories precede the share.
    C9  peers turn to the student conversationally       -> "What about you?" turn in the chat + prompt.
    C10 the elaborate microphone simulation may be
        unnecessary                                     -> dropped; the stories are simply spoken in
        the chat and shown as reference cards, and the entry is a plain written share.
    C11 after the student submits, the tool analyzes the
        story and builds on the writing profile          -> the "circle reflects back" LLM step.
-->

<Vertical id="wj2_activities_part1" title="Writing Journal 2 — Activities" launchable="true">

  <!-- The Export-as-PDF button lives on the wj2_print page at the bottom of this file,
       which writing_journal2b_sba_part1.olx mounts as its "Print &amp; Submit" tab. -->

  <!-- ===================================================================
       INTRO SLIDE
       =================================================================== -->

    <Markdown id="wj2_welcome">
## Writing Journal 2: How Do You *Really* Feel About Writing?

It's the middle of your first-year writing unit, and your group chat with the **Writers' Circle** is
blowing up. **Lee** is stuck on the Literacy Narrative assignment and calling it writer's block.
**Richard** says writer's block isn't even real. **Alma** isn't so sure — something happened to her in
ninth grade that she's never quite shaken.

You don't have to have the answer. This journal is a chance to notice something most writers never stop
to name: **how you actually feel about writing, and where those feelings came from.**

> **The big idea for this journal:** writing is influenced by our identities and prior experiences
> (Wardle and Downs). In other words, how you feel when you sit down to write isn't proof that you're
> "a writer" or "not a writer" — it's a *tendency*, shaped by things that happened to you, and
> tendencies can change.

**By the end of this journal you will have:**

- taken a quick, no-wrong-answers quiz about **how you feel about writing** and **what you think makes
  writing good**;
- seen where the Circle's tool places you on three **attitude continuums** — and decided for yourself
  how accurate that feels;
- listened to three peers tell the story of a moment that shaped them as writers; and
- told **your own story** — the real journal entry — about an experience that shaped how you feel about
  yourself as a writer.

There are no wrong answers and no grade for feeling one way or another about writing. What counts is
honesty. Work through the conversation on the left — each step opens an activity here on the right.
    </Markdown>

