  <!-- ===================================================================
       THE DEBATE: YOUR QUICK TAKE  (activates prior experience; low friction)
       =================================================================== -->

  <Vertical id="wj2_debate_react">
    <Markdown>
## What's Your Take on Writer's Block?

Lee, Richard, and Alma can't agree, and there's no official answer. Before you take the quiz with them,
weigh in: **Do you think writer's block is a real thing — and have you ever experienced it?**

Pick the one that rings most true today. Whatever your view, there's no wrong answer — just go with what
feels natural. (You can always feel differently tomorrow — that's kind of the point.)
    </Markdown>
    <!-- Short semantic values, not the sentences: the prose is display text and gets copy-edited,
         so anything that stores or reads it drifts. Surfaces that show the student their own answer
         (the artifact tab) <Use> this block to re-render the sentence; the LLM prompt that reads the
         value carries a legend for the codes. -->
    <ChoiceInput id="wj2_block_take">
      <Key id="wj2_take_freeze"      value="freeze"     > It's real, and I've felt it — my brain just freezes up, even when I have ideas </Key>
      <Key id="wj2_take_anxiety"     value="anxiety"    > It's real for me, but it feels more like anxiety or fear of getting it wrong   </Key>
      <Key id="wj2_take_not_started" value="not_started"> Richard's kind of right — I'm not sure it's real; I just haven't started yet   </Key>
      <Key id="wj2_take_depends"     value="depends"    > It depends completely on what I'm being asked to write                         </Key>
      <Key id="wj2_take_unsure"      value="unsure"     > I'm honestly not sure yet                                                      </Key>
    </ChoiceInput>
    <Markdown>
*Alma's hunch is that the answer has less to do with willpower and more to do with what happened to you
**before** you ever sat down to write. Hold onto your answer — you'll come back to it.*
    </Markdown>
  </Vertical>

  <!-- ===================================================================
       TASK 1a — "HOW DO YOU REALLY FEEL ABOUT WRITING?"  (26 statements)
       Buzzfeed-style attitudes quiz, ONE STATEMENT AT A TIME. Each item is an
       emoji ChoiceInput; item N is when-gated on the previous item having a
       value, so the next statement only appears once this one is answered.
       A character progress bar ("Question N of 26") sits above each item.
       The chat gates advancing on @s26.value (all 26 answered) so the quiz
       cannot be skipped. Each sNN stores a self-describing agreement value —
       strongly_agree / agree / disagree / strongly_disagree — not the emoji display
       label, so the prose can be copy-edited freely while the stored value still
       reads as English wherever it surfaces (the results prompt, the print page,
       the event stream). The gates below are truthiness-only and don't care about
       the value. The results tool reads the values against the s01-s26 legend it
       carries, which names what each statement measures.
       =================================================================== -->

